## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends classic knapsack-style dynamic programming to incorporate time-based constraints (deadlines) and a limit on the number of selectable jobs. It tests understanding of multi-dimensional DP states, proper handling of time as a resource, and the ability to reconstruct the solution.

## Key Concepts
- Scheduling with deadlines and profits
- 3D DP formulation tracking (jobs processed, jobs selected, time)
- Backward iteration to avoid reusing the same job
- Reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time complexity: O(n × K × D), where D is the maximum deadline

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitJobs(jobs, K) {
  jobs.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d);
  const maxT = Math.max(...jobs.map(j => j.d));
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (const { p, d, w } of jobs) {
    for (let k = K - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      for (let t = d; t >= p; t--) {
        if (dp[k][t - p] !== NEG) {
          const newProfit = dp[k][t - p] + w;
          if (newProfit > dp[k + 1][t]) {
            dp[k + 1][t] = newProfit;
            prev[k + 1][t] = { prevK: k, prevT: t - p };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let bestProfit = 0, bestK = 0, bestT = 0;
  for (let k = 1; k <= K; k++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= maxT; t++) {
      if (dp[k][t] > bestProfit) {
        bestProfit = dp[k][t];
        bestK = k;
        bestT = t;
      }
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let [k, t] = [bestK, bestT];
  while (k > 0) {
    const { prevK, prevT } = prev[k][t];
    selected.push({ finishTime: t });
    k = prevK;
    t = prevT;
  }

  return { profit: bestProfit, jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests advanced dynamic programming techniques involving multiple non-overlapping segments with per-element costs—a pattern frequently appearing in interviews for algorithm-intensive roles.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of blocks requiring optimal segmentation
- Prefix sum transformations to simplify recurrence relations
- Time-space optimization strategies and solution reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValue(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i+1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const B = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let x = 0; x <= n; x++) B[x] = P[x] - c * x;

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i][t-1];
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + B[i] - B[j];
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && parent[curr][currT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[curr][currT];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    currT--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Implementation Details
- The transformation B[x] = P[x] - c*x captures the net value after maintenance costs
- The nested loop structure ensures we consider all possible segment boundaries
- Parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal segment endpoints

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests designing a DP over a scan of the array with extra dimensions to enforce fixed-length, non-overlapping subsequences and reconstructing the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions with two resource dimensions: completed subsequences and in-progress length
- Fixed-length subsequences ensure non-overlap via r reset on completion
- Backtracking reconstructs actual indices

